WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.130
The chatbot planned my fractions

2
00:00:03.130 --> 00:00:05.110
lesson for the wrong school!

3
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:08.940
It assumed a projector, worksheets

4
00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.120
for everyone and thirty pupils.

5
00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:14.410
I have sixty-two pupils, one

6
00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:17.190
textbook, and no power after noon.

7
00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.850
Today: prompts that fit your class.

8
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:23.880
The chatbot wasn’t careless.

9
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.800
It knows nothing about your class: your

10
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.520
pupils’ age, how many, what you have,

11
00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:32.480
the language they think in.

12
00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:36.010
So it predicts the most typical answer: an

13
00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:38.830
average classroom, which is nobody’s.

14
00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.680
A strong prompt has four ingredients.

15
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.380
A role: “You’re a primary

16
00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:46.880
maths teacher in Nigeria.”

17
00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:50.240
A task: “Plan a forty-minute

18
00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.080
lesson on halves and quarters.”

19
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:54.920
Context: “Primary Four,

20
00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.880
sixty-two pupils, one textbook,

21
00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.520
no projector, Hausa at home.”

22
00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.380
And a format: “Numbered steps with

23
00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:05.580
timings, and one exit question.”

24
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.840
When you want a certain style, show one.

25
00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:12.110
“Here’s a word problem my pupils

26
00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:14.770
loved: Musa shares twelve

27
00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:16.410
mangoes with three friends.

28
00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:18.750
Write five more like it.”

29
00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.060
One good example often says more than

30
00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:23.400
a paragraph of instructions.

31
00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:26.930
So, a prompt is what you

32
00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:28.630
tell an AI tool to do.

33
00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:31.410
Its most important part is often the

34
00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:34.270
context: what only you know about your

35
00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:36.150
pupils, room and resources.

36
00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:38.670
That makes the answer fit.

37
00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.180
It’s like briefing a relief teacher

38
00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:43.660
who has never seen your school.

39
00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.180
“Teach fractions” isn’t enough.

40
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.940
You’d say: Primary Four, sixty-two

41
00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:52.780
pupils, one textbook, start with

42
00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:54.760
halves, and the power goes at noon.

43
00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:56.620
Quick check!

44
00:01:57.410 --> 00:01:59.710
Amina’s prompt says: “Write

45
00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.430
ten maths questions.”

46
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.140
What would help most?

47
00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:08.690
Her pupils’ age, level and resources.

48
00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:11.970
Being polite doesn’t hurt, and

49
00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.990
“excellent” sounds good, but neither

50
00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:17.990
says anything about Primary Four in Kano.

51
00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:20.220
Context turns a typical

52
00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.960
answer into one that fits.

53
00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:24.840
When the first answer isn’t

54
00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.520
right, don’t start again.

55
00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:28.980
Follow up, as with a colleague.

56
00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:30.400
“Make it shorter.”

57
00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:33.480
“Use names and foods from Kano.”

58
00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:36.600
“Add a harder version for fast finishers.”

59
00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:40.130
And “Now in Hausa”, then check it

60
00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:41.490
with someone who speaks Hausa.

61
00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:44.680
In the app, build a prompt

62
00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:45.360
from ingredients.

63
00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:49.140
A role and a task are already on.

64
00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:52.430
Add the context, a format and the

65
00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:56.050
language, ask the AI each time, and watch

66
00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:58.470
its water-cycle questions get more useful.

67
00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:00.290
Your turn.

68
00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:03.300
The plan is good, but far too

69
00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:04.520
long for my forty minutes.

70
00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:06.130
What should I do?

71
00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:09.340
Follow up, and ask it to

72
00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:10.400
fit forty minutes.

73
00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:13.650
Remember: the AI can’t see your class.

74
00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.920
Give it a role, a task, context and a

75
00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:20.940
format, and an example when style matters.

76
00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:23.750
Then follow up until it fits.

77
00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:26.490
Try it in class tomorrow:

78
00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:28.370
a prompt makeover.

79
00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:31.470
Show a weak prompt and the answer it got.

80
00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:35.180
Each group adds one ingredient, a role,

81
00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:38.700
the context or a format, and predicts

82
00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:39.920
how the answer will change.

83
00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:42.510
If you have a phone, test one together.
